cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "xfeeds: an independence-aware public threat intelligence feed"
message: "If you use xfeeds or its published feeds in your work, please cite it using the metadata in this file."
type: software
authors:
  - given-names: Neil
    family-names: Weitzel
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0009-0007-2546-2331"
    city: Indianapolis
    region: Indiana
    country: US
repository-code: "https://github.com/neilweitzel/xfeeds"
url: "https://neilweitzel.github.io/xfeeds/"
# Only the concept DOI is listed here, and that is deliberate. A concept DOI is
# version-agnostic — it always resolves to the newest published version — so it
# stays correct no matter what `version` below says. A version-specific DOI does
# not: listing one pins this file to whichever release that DOI archives, which
# would force `version` to lag the pipeline whenever a candidate is not deposited.
#
# The rule going forward: `version` below always equals the version in
# pyproject.toml, and `scripts/check_version_agreement.py` fails CI if it does
# not. A version-specific DOI is added here only for a final release, at which
# point it names the same version as everything else. See ADR-055.
#
# Version DOIs for individual releases are recorded in docs/CITABILITY.md and are
# always discoverable from the concept DOI's Zenodo version list.
identifiers:
  - type: doi
    value: 10.5281/zenodo.22045733
    description: Concept DOI (always resolves to the newest version)
abstract: >-
  xfeeds is a self-updating, open threat intelligence pipeline that collects,
  normalizes, enriches, scores, filters, and publishes malicious-IP indicators
  in interoperable formats including CSV, JSON, STIX 2.1, MISP, and firewall
  rule sets. Unlike naive aggregation of public blocklists, which manufactures
  false confidence because many public lists mirror or re-derive one another,
  xfeeds models source dependency explicitly. Sources are assigned independence
  classes and only the strongest contribution within a class counts toward
  corroboration, while freshness gating prevents dormant observations from
  independently promoting an indicator. Redistribution rights are enforced
  structurally: restricted sources may raise confidence without appearing in
  published records, and separate non-commercial and clean-provenance feed
  tiers carry distinct redistribution promises.
keywords:
  - threat intelligence
  - indicators of compromise
  - IP blocklist
  - data provenance
  - source independence
  - STIX
  - open data
  - network security
license: MIT
# Must equal pyproject.toml's version. PEP 440 requires `1.0.0rc6` there and the
# git tag uses `v1.0.0-rc.6`; these are the same version in two mandated
# spellings, and CI compares them on a canonical form rather than as strings.
version: 1.0.0-rc.6
date-released: "2026-09-01"
